WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.519 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Does a longer decimal always mean a bigger
number?

2
00:00:03.274 --> 00:00:06.177 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
No. Compare place values instead.

3
00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:10.553 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Start with the whole number parts, then
tenths, hundredths and

4
00:00:10.623 --> 00:00:14.652 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
thousandths. Adding zeros at the end of a
decimal can

5
00:00:14.721 --> 00:00:17.578 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
help line up the places without changing
its value.

6
00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:21.096 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Compare one point four zero seven and one
point four

7
00:00:21.165 --> 00:00:24.544 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
seven. Write the second as one point four
seven zero.

8
00:00:25.229 --> 00:00:26.611 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Ones and tenths match.

9
00:00:27.402 --> 00:00:30.374 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
At the hundredths place, zero is less than
seven.

10
00:00:30.815 --> 00:00:33.637 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
So one point four zero seven is less than
one

11
00:00:33.695 --> 00:00:34.867 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
point four seven zero.

12
00:00:35.308 --> 00:00:38.919 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
For ordering, line up two point zero five
three, two

13
00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:42.286 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
point three zero five, and two point three
five zero.

14
00:00:42.727 --> 00:00:46.315 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
The first is smallest because its tenths
digit is zero.

15
00:00:46.814 --> 00:00:50.715 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
The other two match in tenths, but zero
hundredths is

16
00:00:50.831 --> 00:00:52.154 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
less than five hundredths.

17
00:00:52.596 --> 00:00:54.569 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Now locate a number on a line.

18
00:00:54.935 --> 00:00:58.372 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
From one point four zero to one point five
zero,

19
00:00:58.615 --> 00:01:01.425 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
divide the interval into ten equal steps.

20
00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.201 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each step is one hundredth.

21
00:01:03.573 --> 00:01:06.382 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
One point four three and one point four
four are

22
00:01:06.452 --> 00:01:08.426 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
neighbouring marks on this line.

23
00:01:08.844 --> 00:01:13.569 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
To locate one point four three seven more
precisely, zoom

24
00:01:13.662 --> 00:01:17.052 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
in between one point four three and one
point four

25
00:01:17.145 --> 00:01:20.825 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
four. Divide that interval into ten equal
steps.

26
00:01:21.243 --> 00:01:23.113 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Each step is one thousandth.

27
00:01:23.617 --> 00:01:26.380 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Count seven steps from one point four
three zero to

28
00:01:26.461 --> 00:01:28.075 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
reach one point four three seven.

29
00:01:28.482 --> 00:01:31.674 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Finally, four point seven equals four
point seven zero zero:

30
00:01:32.116 --> 00:01:34.821 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
both represent seven tenths after four
wholes.

31
00:01:35.227 --> 00:01:37.932 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Extra end zeros do not add any value.

32
00:01:38.222 --> 00:01:41.520 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
Remember: line up the places, compare from
the left, and

33
00:01:41.566 --> 00:01:44.306 line:1% position:50% align:center size:90%
check the equal step size before locating
a decimal.

